Compute Engine in now GA.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:22.550
So what that means is when you
remote into your actual server,

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:25.250
your windows instance,
you can actually

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:27.770
create a virtual
display specifically

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:31.130
for your VM that's running
its own separate CPU, which

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:32.750
is kind of awesome,
because you don't

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:34.350
have to allocate GPUs for it.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:36.500
So that means that this
can be less expensive.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:37.550
MARK MANDEL: Very nice.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:38.300
Awesome.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:42.650
Speaking of going GA, cloud
native load balancing on GKE

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:44.510
is now generally available.

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:46.010
So if you're not
familiar with that,

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:48.552
basically if you want to do load
balancing to your containers

00:01:48.552 --> 00:01:52.350
running on top of GKE, this is a
more efficient way of doing so.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:55.760
So this is basically what they
call network endpoint group

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:56.385
load balancing.

00:01:56.385 --> 00:01:58.885
Basically it means that your
traffic is going from your load

00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:01.250
balancer directly to your
container, rather than sort

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.050
of meandering its way
around your cluster

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:04.442
to eventually end up at a pod.

00:02:04.442 --> 00:02:06.650
We'll have a link in the
show notes to the blog post.

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:08.759
They have a great diagram
explaining this really well.

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:11.170
But yeah, if you want to take
it for a spin, it's now GA.

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:12.253
JON FOUST: That's awesome.

