00:03:47.135 --> 00:03:50.990
So yeah, I started Phoenix
Labs about five years ago now.

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:54.700
We have been basically from the
beginning making "Dauntless."

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:58.280
"Dauntless" has been out
in its most recent form.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:02.000
It's got a wide release,
full access form since May.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:03.980
So we launched on May 21.

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:06.980
Prior to that, we did various
kind of rounds of betas

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:10.310
from an open beta last year,
and then the year prior to that,

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:11.870
we kind of had a closed beta.

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:14.630
So "Dauntless" has actually been
around for about three years,

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:17.138
just kind of in various
soft launched forms.

00:04:17.138 --> 00:04:18.680
Just kind of give
you an idea of what

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:21.720
"Dauntless" is because I always
speak like everybody knows.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:23.390
So it's called a
hunting action game.

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:26.150
It's about you and your
friends getting together

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:30.440
on any platform, coming
together, fighting big monsters

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:31.743
that we call behemoths.

00:04:31.743 --> 00:04:33.410
And then you kind of
collect their bits,

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:35.440
and you go back into the
city, and you hang out,

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.160
and you forge new
weapons and armor.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:39.118
And you go back and kill
bigger, scarier-- kind

00:04:39.118 --> 00:04:40.682
of these big boss encounters.

00:04:40.682 --> 00:04:42.140
JON FOUST: I remember
the open beta

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:44.930
when it was at Google
Cloud Next last year.

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:46.010
That was my first Next.

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:47.210
So I do remember the beta.

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:49.610
You did mention that
you can pretty much

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:52.460
play with your friends
on any platform.

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:54.320
So I'm assuming
that's cross-platform.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:54.650
JESSE HOUSTON: Yeah.

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:55.400
JON FOUST: Right?

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:56.900
JESSE HOUSTON: Yeah,
so we're, like,

00:04:56.900 --> 00:05:00.950
the third game ever to allow
what we call kind of the "one

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:02.110
'Dauntless' experience."

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:05.900
So the idea of, if you're on
PC and I'm on Xbox and Mark

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:08.110
is on PS4, we could
all play together,

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:10.070
and that you guys
could switch consoles,

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:11.720
and it would be
totally just fine.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:15.152
It's a pretty crazy thing,
but we love the fact

00:05:15.152 --> 00:05:16.610
that not only can
we play together,

00:05:16.610 --> 00:05:18.660
but we can be on each
other's friends lists.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.690
And we can swap back and
forth and that kind of stuff.

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:22.607
MARK MANDEL: Just to be
clear, I'm a PC gamer.

00:05:22.607 --> 00:05:23.590
JESSE HOUSTON: Good.

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:24.690
MARK MANDEL: Not to
disparage other people

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:26.090
playing console, I just--

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:28.130
I can't play games
on the controller.

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:28.940
I can't do it.

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:30.023
JESSE HOUSTON: It's funny.

00:05:30.023 --> 00:05:32.232
I actually play "Dauntless"
with a controller on PC.

00:05:32.232 --> 00:05:33.440
MARK MANDEL: Oh, interesting.

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:34.160
Interesting.

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:35.690
There are certain types
of games like that,

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:37.607
I just end up looking
at the sky or the floor,

00:05:37.607 --> 00:05:39.240
and that's just my life.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:40.460
JESSE HOUSTON: Totally.

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:40.760
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:41.000
JESSE HOUSTON: I feel you.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:43.850
MARK MANDEL: So you said
you've been running "Dauntless"

00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:45.517
for over three years.

00:05:45.517 --> 00:05:46.850
It's an online multiplayer game.

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:48.358
So us in the industry-- oh god.

00:05:48.358 --> 00:05:49.400
I don't want to say that.

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:49.942
That's awful.

00:05:49.942 --> 00:05:52.940
But we refer to that as
kind of like a live service

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:53.525
online game.

00:05:53.525 --> 00:05:54.400
It's an ongoing game.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:55.190
What's that like?

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:56.960
Can you tell us a little bit
about what is a live service

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.330
game, and what is the
experience of running that like?

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:00.913
JESSE HOUSTON: We
knew from the get-go

00:06:00.913 --> 00:06:02.960
that we wanted to be
a live service game.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:04.710
Yeah, we call it a
live service game, too.

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:08.240
And so literally from the
first line of code written,

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:10.910
or the first submit,
anyways, under Perforce,

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:13.010
had to come with
a deployment step.

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:15.470
And so we started running
dedicated servers, like,

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:17.010
four years ago.

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:18.772
Initially, it was a total mess.

00:06:18.772 --> 00:06:20.480
We do these things
called holiday builds,

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:22.147
or we used to do them
when we were still

00:06:22.147 --> 00:06:24.710
in a really closed state where
we try and get everything

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:26.240
in just before the holidays.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:30.380
And then while we did the studio
closure, everybody would play.

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:34.310
And I can remember in, I think
it was 2015's holiday build,

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:35.960
myself and the
technical director

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:38.445
basically get 12 hour shifts
through the entire holiday--

00:06:38.445 --> 00:06:39.320
JON FOUST: Oh my god.

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:41.195
JESSE HOUSTON: --writing
Python scripts, just

00:06:41.195 --> 00:06:42.920
to keep the game from
falling over with,

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:45.350
like, 150 people playing.

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:46.810
Not at the same
time-- like period.

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:50.420
[CHUCKLING] There would be,
like, 10 people playing,

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:54.080
and we'd have to do manual
log rotations and all

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:55.130
this other stuff.

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:58.280
That kind of experience really
taught us all of the things

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:01.080
that we needed kind of
progressively as we launched.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:04.560
And so there's a lot of stuff
on May 21 that went wrong,

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:07.700
but a lot of stuff that actually
went really right because we

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:10.250
had been doing it kind
of progressively bigger.

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:13.250
And having never turned the
servers off in four years

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:15.400
really helps kind of
just get used to it.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:18.105
A lot of companies that go
into the live environment

00:07:18.105 --> 00:07:20.730
tend to have to all of a sudden
slow down because they're like,

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:22.020
oh, what is updating?

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:24.050
Whereas it's no big deal.

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:26.330
In the last two weeks,
we've patched five times,

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:28.950
and most players only saw
one patch, for example.

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:30.950
A lot of our stuff doesn't
need to take the game

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:32.220
servers down all the time.

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:34.430
And it's been a great learning
experience either way.

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:35.972
JON FOUST: That's
the best way to go,

00:07:35.972 --> 00:07:37.910
not having to actually
turn off your game

00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:38.960
in order to get a patch.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:40.640
It doesn't really
mean any downtime

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:42.778
because I hate those games
where you have updates.

00:07:42.778 --> 00:07:44.570
You have to wait three
hours, and I'm like,

00:07:44.570 --> 00:07:46.080
now what do I do with my life?

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:48.080
JESSE HOUSTON: It's been really
interesting, that experience,

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:48.860
by the way.

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:52.580
Xbox and Playstation
don't have tools

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:55.640
to support that kind of
experience of the no downtime

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:56.570
deploys.

00:07:56.570 --> 00:07:59.630
And so we've gotten the
opportunity to work with them

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:02.120
and kind of do a little
bit of teaching on our own

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:04.635
on what that experience can
feel like, and that it's OK,

00:08:04.635 --> 00:08:06.260
and that that doesn't
mean that there's

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:08.450
going to be rampant
hacking or some sort

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:09.680
of other vulnerabilities.

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:12.758
Downtime and deploying don't
need to be the same thing.

00:08:12.758 --> 00:08:15.050
And it's actually been a
really cool experience getting

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:17.930
to kind of introduce
gamers on these platforms

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:18.823
to that experience.

00:08:18.823 --> 00:08:20.740
JON FOUST: Let's go back
a little bit and talk

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:23.995
about going from 150
gamers to a full launch.

00:08:23.995 --> 00:08:25.930
And let's talk about scaling.

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:28.150
Can you tell us a
little bit how your team

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:31.750
went about scaling this to
support a full time launch?

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:34.870
And then you've
gone from 150 to God

00:08:34.870 --> 00:08:36.549
knows how many
players you have now.

00:08:36.549 --> 00:08:39.973
JESSE HOUSTON: Initially, when
we did everything, it was all--

00:08:39.973 --> 00:08:41.140
everything was super manual.

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:45.040
So we would deploy a VM,
manually upload a build,

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:47.890
and initially it was
on Windows boxes only.

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:52.090
And it was all put your IPs in
by hand and that kind of stuff.

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:54.580
And then we basically
kind of just

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:58.210
kept iterating to add
more and more automation

00:08:58.210 --> 00:08:59.480
through the processes.

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:01.380
And then as you're
flexing that and as

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:04.060
you're adding more players,
stuff just kind of breaks.

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:06.820
And so a lot of our
kind of scaling efforts

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:09.820
were around just
fixing what was broken

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:13.420
and making sure that we're then
able to see into the future

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:15.610
far enough that we
could kind of see

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:17.050
the next most vulnerable thing.

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:19.793
Like more recently
with SQL databases,

00:09:19.793 --> 00:09:20.710
there was a bunch of--

00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:24.880
our inventory was on SQL, and
we had millions and millions

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:26.220
of players showing up every day.

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:28.480
And the transaction
rates were way too high

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:30.640
for whatever SQL
was ever going to be

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:33.430
able to handle until we were
able to move over to Bigtable.

00:09:33.430 --> 00:09:35.900
And we had to do that in a
no-downtime world as well.

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:38.500
So that was super
exciting, having

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:40.940
to write basically a service
that would check Bigtable.

00:09:40.940 --> 00:09:42.940
And if it wasn't there,
then go get it from SQL,

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:44.120
and then load it
into a Bigtable,

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:45.912
and then give the
response back to players,

00:09:45.912 --> 00:09:47.240
and that kind of stuff.

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:48.997
And so through that
whole experience,

00:09:48.997 --> 00:09:50.830
though, we've basically
been like, OK, well,

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:52.930
that's the next thing
that's going to fall over.

00:09:52.930 --> 00:09:56.380
Let's go and harden
it or re-architect it

00:09:56.380 --> 00:09:57.680
or what have you.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:02.740
And we've gone from
hardcore VM-based 2014,

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:06.550
2015 thought processes on how
stateful game servers should

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:07.500
run to--

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:09.852
almost everything now
runs in Kubernetes.

00:10:09.852 --> 00:10:12.310
We were able to basically load
a bunch of stateful services

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:14.643
into that, and now all of our
game servers, for example,

00:10:14.643 --> 00:10:15.450
are in CAdES.

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:18.340
And that's been hugely
helpful, because we

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:21.070
don't have to worry about
things like VM management

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:21.730
nearly as much.

00:10:21.730 --> 00:10:22.772
It's just like, OK, cool.

00:10:22.772 --> 00:10:23.560
Fire up a pod.

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:24.760
Put some players on it.

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:26.390
If the pod has a
problem, no big deal.

00:10:26.390 --> 00:10:27.130
It can die.

00:10:27.130 --> 00:10:30.400
And it doesn't take down
dozens and dozens of players.

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:35.110
It only affects four, whereas,
historically, if a VM died--

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:37.730
which would happen
once or twice a day--

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:39.890
it could take down
thousands of players.

00:10:39.890 --> 00:10:41.650
And so we don't have
that problem anymore.

00:10:41.650 --> 00:10:43.600
So we've been super
lucky that we've just

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:45.580
been able to iterate that way.

00:10:45.580 --> 00:10:46.330
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:47.663
Can you give us a sense of the--

00:10:47.663 --> 00:10:49.180
yeah-- Jon mentioned, scale.

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:52.840
Can you share numbers about how
big of a player base you have,

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:54.130
or at least something general?

00:10:54.130 --> 00:10:54.963
JESSE HOUSTON: Yeah.

00:10:54.963 --> 00:10:56.370
So I think we've announced it.

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:58.360
So hopefully we
have-- otherwise my PR

00:10:58.360 --> 00:11:00.485
guy is going to come at me
with the lightning bolt.

00:11:00.485 --> 00:11:03.100
But we've got about 15 million
players who are actively

00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:05.390
playing "Dauntless," a
great group of folks.

00:11:05.390 --> 00:11:07.240
And that's across
the three platforms,

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:09.118
and we're moving into
Switch next, which

00:11:09.118 --> 00:11:11.410
we're really excited about
all those new players coming

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:13.780
in, and then mobile kind
of in the next year or so.

00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:14.370
MARK MANDEL: Fantastic.

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:14.500
JON FOUST: Yeah.

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:16.120
I'm actually getting
it on Switch.

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:17.740
JESSE HOUSTON: Oh,
dude, it's so good.

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:20.202
Surprisingly great experience.

00:11:20.202 --> 00:11:21.160
MARK MANDEL: So, yeah--

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:23.250
I mean, you've mentioned
dedicated game servers.

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:23.650
JESSE HOUSTON: Mm-hmm.

00:11:23.650 --> 00:11:24.760
MARK MANDEL: Do
you have a variety

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:26.350
of different back-end services?

00:11:26.350 --> 00:11:28.630
Or what does that architecture
look like a little bit?

00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:31.970
JESSE HOUSTON: So it's
entirely inside of GCP,

00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:34.380
and we basically have--

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:35.890
when a user logs
in, there's a bunch

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:39.550
of various accommodation
of both REST based

00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:43.060
and gRPC calls that happen
to various services.

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:47.590
And those are a combination of
Java and Python microservices

00:11:47.590 --> 00:11:49.330
that live in CAdES.

00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:51.970
And then, once you've gone
through all that stuff,

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:57.040
we drop you on to a big, heavy
C++ based game server that

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:01.000
manages most of what you
experience as a player from

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:04.248
what the behemoth is doing and
where you're at, what the city

00:12:04.248 --> 00:12:05.540
looks like, that kind of thing.

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:07.150
And so each of
those pods will have

00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:12.180
anywhere from 4 to 50 players
on them at any given time.

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:13.600
And there are two big ones.

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:16.360
There's the concept of a city,
and then an island server.

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:19.120
And they're different, because
one requires a behemoth,

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:21.343
and the other one
requires lots of players,

00:12:21.343 --> 00:12:23.260
and so we kind of keep
them somewhat separate.

00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:25.930
And then the other is this Cloud
of other services around it,

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:28.360
from inventory to
store management

00:12:28.360 --> 00:12:33.197
to persistent data storage
to telemetry and analytics

00:12:33.197 --> 00:12:34.030
and stuff like that.

00:12:34.030 --> 00:12:37.030
And largely we
shim a lot of calls

00:12:37.030 --> 00:12:38.800
from basically the
client and the servers,

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:40.750
and we kind of farm
it out to other Google

00:12:40.750 --> 00:12:43.678
services from BigQuery
and that kind of stuff.

00:12:43.678 --> 00:12:45.220
JON FOUST: I would
imagine that there

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:47.950
has to be some type of
reasoning for taking

00:12:47.950 --> 00:12:49.660
this type of approach
and going for it

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:51.095
using these back-end services.

00:12:51.095 --> 00:12:53.470
So I'm not sure if you want
to share your secret sauce as

00:12:53.470 --> 00:12:55.540
to why you made
these design choices.

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:56.957
JESSE HOUSTON:
We're pretty lucky.

00:12:56.957 --> 00:13:00.330
This isn't the first time that
we have built a scalable game

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:00.830
service.

00:13:00.830 --> 00:13:02.150
We've got a lot of folks from--

00:13:02.150 --> 00:13:03.610
myself-- from Riot.

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:04.930
We've got some folks from EA.

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:06.310
We've got some
folks from PopCap.

00:13:06.310 --> 00:13:09.640
And so we kind of brought
together a bunch of learnings

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:13.404
from previous big
AAA experiences.

00:13:13.404 --> 00:13:17.740
And as we thought about how
we've historically built stuff,

00:13:17.740 --> 00:13:19.330
there's always
those lists you make

00:13:19.330 --> 00:13:21.705
at the end of any given product
cycle, where you're like,

00:13:21.705 --> 00:13:22.300
man, I wish--

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:24.650
next time, I would
totally do it like this.

00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:28.230
And the forefront of that has
always been horizontal scaling.

00:13:28.230 --> 00:13:30.880
If I look back to some
of my previous games,

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:33.850
there's always that one service
that's super monolithic,

00:13:33.850 --> 00:13:38.560
and we try and just throw
more RAM at it, or more CPUs,

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:40.600
and there's just no way.

00:13:40.600 --> 00:13:42.350
MARK MANDEL: Heard
this story a few times.

00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:43.710
JESSE HOUSTON: Yeah, right.

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:44.080
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:44.950
JESSE HOUSTON: We've
done that, too.

00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:45.940
MARK MANDEL: Just
rewrite it in C++.

00:13:45.940 --> 00:13:46.390
It's fine.

00:13:46.390 --> 00:13:47.080
JESSE HOUSTON: Yeah, exactly.

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:47.580
Right.

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:50.320
Like there's a couple of
Redis boxes lying around

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:54.347
in our platform still that are
very warm at any given time.

00:13:54.347 --> 00:13:56.180
And that's not a knock
on Redis, by the way.

00:13:56.180 --> 00:13:58.125
Redis is amazing and
has lots of good uses.

00:13:58.125 --> 00:14:00.250
We've just been like, ah,
it just scales infinitely

00:14:00.250 --> 00:14:01.457
on that one thread.

00:14:01.457 --> 00:14:03.466
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:03.466 --> 00:14:07.450
And so as we came together
and architected it,

00:14:07.450 --> 00:14:11.020
we knew that we wanted to get a
lot more horizontal scaling in.

00:14:11.020 --> 00:14:14.160
And so we just kind of
keep looking for that.

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:20.920
And also, if you think back to
the 2012 days and through 2015,

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:23.290
the industry was still
very much nascent

00:14:23.290 --> 00:14:24.990
in terms of cloud hosting.

00:14:24.990 --> 00:14:28.040
It was still
considered a bad idea.

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:31.540
And especially on stuff
like the idea of dev ops.

00:14:31.540 --> 00:14:33.542
I'm trying to remember
when the Phoenix

00:14:33.542 --> 00:14:34.750
Project was actually written.

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:38.320
But the idea of dev ops, for
example, was completely unknown

00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:40.360
and things like
continuous delivery

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:42.730
in terms of a live
product service

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:45.600
were still only things that
Google and Facebook could do.

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:48.430
And as an indie
studio, there's no way.

00:14:48.430 --> 00:14:50.920
And we've been really
lucky in that we're now

00:14:50.920 --> 00:14:53.800
at of time in the industry
where a lot of those processes

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:56.320
are well thought
through, and they make

00:14:56.320 --> 00:14:58.600
sense in core product loops.

00:14:58.600 --> 00:15:03.640
And players are also willing
to get into the mindset of,

00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:07.030
oh, if I give feedback
today, tomorrow, I might see

00:15:07.030 --> 00:15:09.410
that change, or even faster.

00:15:09.410 --> 00:15:11.630
So we did a big
deploy yesterday,

00:15:11.630 --> 00:15:14.510
and one of the
players was like, hey,

00:15:14.510 --> 00:15:17.110
this one quest line
doesn't unlock this thing.

00:15:17.110 --> 00:15:19.600
And 30 minutes later,
we deployed the fix,

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:21.680
and he was like, oh, wow.

00:15:21.680 --> 00:15:22.824
That's bloody amazing.

00:15:22.824 --> 00:15:25.060
And it's like, nah, that's
the cool part of what

00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:26.780
2019 can provide.

00:15:26.780 --> 00:15:27.530
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:15:27.530 --> 00:15:28.905
As a particular
interest of mine,

00:15:28.905 --> 00:15:31.870
personally, you chose Kubernetes
and GKE, specifically, even,

00:15:31.870 --> 00:15:33.880
for running dedicated
game servers, which

00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:36.490
is not publicly something
that people talk about.

00:15:36.490 --> 00:15:38.437
Why did you make that decision?

00:15:38.437 --> 00:15:40.270
JESSE HOUSTON: If I'm
being honest with you,

00:15:40.270 --> 00:15:41.020
it's because I'm cheap.

00:15:41.020 --> 00:15:42.562
MARK MANDEL: That's
not a bad reason.

00:15:42.562 --> 00:15:45.377
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:45.377 --> 00:15:46.960
JESSE HOUSTON: One
of the big problems

00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:51.613
that come with game server
hosting is the buffer warmth.

00:15:51.613 --> 00:15:53.780
There's all the game servers
that you've got in use,

00:15:53.780 --> 00:15:55.572
and then stuff that's
currently turned off,

00:15:55.572 --> 00:15:57.590
and then stuff
that's not in use,

00:15:57.590 --> 00:16:00.040
but has to be turned on to
handle any kind of matchmaking

00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:01.450
spikes that happen.

00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:04.600
And my historical
life has taught me

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:08.230
that if I'm doing this
in stack-and-rack land,

00:16:08.230 --> 00:16:10.660
I have to be able to have
enough capacity for my spikes,

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:12.160
and I have to leave them
turned on all the time,

00:16:12.160 --> 00:16:14.120
and I'm paying the bills
and all these boxes.

00:16:14.120 --> 00:16:17.620
And if I'm living in
VM land, VMs can take--

00:16:17.620 --> 00:16:20.110
initially, a Windows
VM could take up

00:16:20.110 --> 00:16:22.180
to two or three
minutes to provision,

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:24.760
and so we always had to
have five minutes of buffer

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:25.898
at any given time.

00:16:25.898 --> 00:16:27.940
And if you think about
"Dauntless," "Dauntless's"

00:16:27.940 --> 00:16:30.680
experience can be as low as
three-minute game sessions,

00:16:30.680 --> 00:16:34.540
so waiting around for five
minutes to have capacity

00:16:34.540 --> 00:16:35.180
is really bad.

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:37.390
So we have to keep quite a
bit more buffer than that.

00:16:37.390 --> 00:16:39.880
And things like garbage
collection-like services

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:41.380
that we would install
on these boxes

00:16:41.380 --> 00:16:44.483
can take a while, just cleaning
up logs and getting replays

00:16:44.483 --> 00:16:46.150
off the boxes, and
all this other stuff,

00:16:46.150 --> 00:16:48.580
and making sure everything's
in the same state.

00:16:48.580 --> 00:16:50.830
Those processes could take
upwards of five minutes

00:16:50.830 --> 00:16:51.420
as well.

00:16:51.420 --> 00:16:56.200
And so we'd have to keep 10%
or 15% extra capacity online

00:16:56.200 --> 00:16:59.410
at any given time, whereas
in GKE, I basically

00:16:59.410 --> 00:17:01.900
can keep like 1%
extra capacity online.

00:17:01.900 --> 00:17:05.020
I just need to make sure
that there are no completely

00:17:05.020 --> 00:17:06.550
full boxes at all.

00:17:06.550 --> 00:17:09.790
And given how rapid the
pods come on and offline,

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:11.980
there's a nice
automatic load balancing

00:17:11.980 --> 00:17:13.613
that ends up happening.

00:17:13.613 --> 00:17:14.530
And so I've actually--

00:17:14.530 --> 00:17:16.910
I just keep a tiny
little bit of buffer.

00:17:16.910 --> 00:17:19.160
And it's also super
nice-- it spills over.

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:21.910
So if we get a crazy
spike, like if an outage

00:17:21.910 --> 00:17:24.849
happens, for example, in
one region, no big deal.

00:17:24.849 --> 00:17:26.020
Players can spill over.

00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:27.790
We can very, very,
very, very quickly

00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:29.712
spin up capacity
elsewhere, and there

00:17:29.712 --> 00:17:32.170
can be a really nice, tight
integration with our matchmaker

00:17:32.170 --> 00:17:33.658
so that players
don't even notice.

00:17:33.658 --> 00:17:34.450
JON FOUST: Awesome.

00:17:34.450 --> 00:17:37.810
So I'm curious, because
when "Dauntless" launched--

00:17:37.810 --> 00:17:39.715
I'm assuming that--
now, we're in 2019,

00:17:39.715 --> 00:17:41.740
and everything's
running in the cloud.

00:17:41.740 --> 00:17:44.140
What is the trickiest
part of scaling a game?

00:17:44.140 --> 00:17:47.248
I've seen launches go off,
and then immediately servers

00:17:47.248 --> 00:17:49.290
went down because they
were anticipating numbers.

00:17:49.290 --> 00:17:50.860
JESSE HOUSTON: Oh,
that happened with us.

00:17:50.860 --> 00:17:51.880
MARK MANDEL: Yes,
talk us through it.

00:17:51.880 --> 00:17:54.190
JESSE HOUSTON: We had all
sorts of fun, exciting times.

00:17:54.190 --> 00:17:56.180
I mean, so we knew a lot more
players are going to show up.

00:17:56.180 --> 00:17:58.540
You go into consoles, there's
kind of like a validation

00:17:58.540 --> 00:17:59.230
moment that happens.

00:17:59.230 --> 00:18:00.510
We've got all this
PR rolling up.

00:18:00.510 --> 00:18:01.550
We've got all these influencers.

00:18:01.550 --> 00:18:03.883
So we know that there's a
bunch more players showing up.

00:18:03.883 --> 00:18:06.730
And the problem that games
have that I love, but I hate,

00:18:06.730 --> 00:18:09.340
is that there's not really a
nice, easy way of load testing

00:18:09.340 --> 00:18:10.970
without real humans.

00:18:10.970 --> 00:18:13.510
And so finding a
quarter million humans

00:18:13.510 --> 00:18:16.450
or whatever to come and log
into a game before it's launched

00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:17.900
is literally impossible.

00:18:17.900 --> 00:18:20.720
And so we needed to basically
think about the new way.

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:23.140
And so we did all of the
usual stuff with load testing,

00:18:23.140 --> 00:18:25.480
like every service has
a Locust thing that

00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:28.360
just blaps the living heck
out of every end-point.

00:18:28.360 --> 00:18:30.650
And we saw what we
thought our load could be.

00:18:30.650 --> 00:18:32.150
But that's still
not representative,

00:18:32.150 --> 00:18:34.600
and so we were able to
actually basically analyze

00:18:34.600 --> 00:18:36.610
what real players did
in the closed beta,

00:18:36.610 --> 00:18:39.590
and use that data
to inform bots.

00:18:39.590 --> 00:18:41.650
And then cool parts
about Kubernetes

00:18:41.650 --> 00:18:44.800
is we were then able to spin up
literally hundreds of thousands

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:47.600
of bots to play
"Dauntless" headless.

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:50.830
They were still real accounts,
just they didn't have graphics.

00:18:50.830 --> 00:18:52.872
And they knew what to do
because they represented

00:18:52.872 --> 00:18:54.247
real-life players,
and so we were

00:18:54.247 --> 00:18:56.230
able to do just spin up
thousands and thousands

00:18:56.230 --> 00:18:57.790
and thousands of them.

00:18:57.790 --> 00:19:00.760
And so that actually got us a
ton of really good information.

00:19:00.760 --> 00:19:02.950
And then it started
shutting off our video game,

00:19:02.950 --> 00:19:04.310
and we couldn't figure out why.

00:19:04.310 --> 00:19:07.300
And so the Thursday
before launch,

00:19:07.300 --> 00:19:08.980
every time that we
did a bot test that

00:19:08.980 --> 00:19:11.622
had more than 40,000
people playing in it,

00:19:11.622 --> 00:19:12.580
it would shut off prod.

00:19:12.580 --> 00:19:13.420
MARK MANDEL: Ouch.

00:19:13.420 --> 00:19:15.100
JESSE HOUSTON: We'd be running
it in a different environment,

00:19:15.100 --> 00:19:17.058
and then, all of a sudden,
prod would shut off,

00:19:17.058 --> 00:19:18.610
and we couldn't figure out why.

00:19:18.610 --> 00:19:21.220
Turns out, by the way, that
when you write a botnet,

00:19:21.220 --> 00:19:23.883
anti-botnet protections
that you put in place work.

00:19:23.883 --> 00:19:24.550
MARK MANDEL: Oh.

00:19:24.550 --> 00:19:26.630
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:26.630 --> 00:19:29.200
And a whole bunch of game
players looks like a botnet.

00:19:29.200 --> 00:19:30.500
JESSE HOUSTON: Yeah, exactly.

00:19:30.500 --> 00:19:33.490
So that took a long time for us
to figure out what was wrong.

00:19:33.490 --> 00:19:36.010
These groups that you work with
to write these protections,

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:39.110
part of how they do it is they
like cut off all communication.

00:19:39.110 --> 00:19:42.310
Yeah, so we basically DDOSed
ourselves into submission right

00:19:42.310 --> 00:19:44.080
through the day before launch.

00:19:44.080 --> 00:19:46.590
Finally got that fixed.

00:19:46.590 --> 00:19:49.330
The fact is the play
patterns changed,

00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:51.850
and a lot more people showed
up than we ever expected--

00:19:51.850 --> 00:19:53.680
probably three or
four times more folks

00:19:53.680 --> 00:19:56.080
showed up than any
of our expectations.

00:19:56.080 --> 00:19:57.250
MARK MANDEL: Congratulations

00:19:57.250 --> 00:19:57.850
JESSE HOUSTON: Thank you.

00:19:57.850 --> 00:19:58.270
Yeah.

00:19:58.270 --> 00:19:59.997
Great first-world
problem to have, right?

00:19:59.997 --> 00:20:02.080
But we're really, really,
really, really concerned

00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:03.720
with delivering a
great experience,

00:20:03.720 --> 00:20:07.120
and so a lot of what we were
trying to do at that point

00:20:07.120 --> 00:20:09.910
then was trying to
get ourselves back up

00:20:09.910 --> 00:20:11.710
and running as fast as
possible, setting up

00:20:11.710 --> 00:20:14.230
things like Bigtable, and
moving a lot of our databases

00:20:14.230 --> 00:20:16.525
over to Bigtable,
as well as trying

00:20:16.525 --> 00:20:17.650
to communicate effectively.

00:20:17.650 --> 00:20:19.440
And then inching up-- cool.

00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:21.310
Well, what if we let
10,000 more players in?

00:20:21.310 --> 00:20:21.850
All right, cool.

00:20:21.850 --> 00:20:22.600
We inched that up.

00:20:22.600 --> 00:20:25.150
OK, what's the next thing that
starts to become unstable?

00:20:25.150 --> 00:20:27.040
The launch week between
Tuesday to Tuesday,

00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:29.500
we did 1712 deploys.

00:20:29.500 --> 00:20:30.240
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:32.407
I thought you were-- just
kind of watching this from

00:20:32.407 --> 00:20:34.360
the sidelines as a
fan of GCP in games--

00:20:34.360 --> 00:20:35.075
and I thought that
you did something

00:20:35.075 --> 00:20:37.283
that I thought was actually
really cool in terms of--

00:20:37.283 --> 00:20:39.280
you capture players
at certain points,

00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:40.960
and then only allowed
certain numbers in

00:20:40.960 --> 00:20:41.860
and had a queuing system.

00:20:41.860 --> 00:20:42.040
JESSE HOUSTON: Yeah.

00:20:42.040 --> 00:20:42.490
MARK MANDEL: That
was something I

00:20:42.490 --> 00:20:43.970
don't think I've seen
many other people do,

00:20:43.970 --> 00:20:44.910
and I thought that was
really interesting.

00:20:44.910 --> 00:20:46.230
Can you talk us through that?

00:20:46.230 --> 00:20:46.780
JESSE HOUSTON: Some
of the other games

00:20:46.780 --> 00:20:48.655
that I've worked on in
the past, what ends up

00:20:48.655 --> 00:20:51.280
happening when the services
get full is they just kind

00:20:51.280 --> 00:20:53.740
of keep layering them
in there and having

00:20:53.740 --> 00:20:56.320
a semi-degraded service
until the whole thing

00:20:56.320 --> 00:20:57.400
kind of cascades over.

00:20:57.400 --> 00:20:59.770
And so our thought
process was, what

00:20:59.770 --> 00:21:04.210
if there was a very thin
version of the game that

00:21:04.210 --> 00:21:06.970
spoke just enough of the
language of the servers

00:21:06.970 --> 00:21:09.250
to basically leave
them in a queue,

00:21:09.250 --> 00:21:12.532
and have enough call homes
that scaled wildly until--

00:21:12.532 --> 00:21:13.990
and then what if
we made it so that

00:21:13.990 --> 00:21:18.520
could take 100 times our
wildest dreams of players?

00:21:18.520 --> 00:21:20.453
The queue itself,
for example, is--

00:21:20.453 --> 00:21:21.370
coming back to Redis--

00:21:21.370 --> 00:21:23.710
it was basically a
little Python app that

00:21:23.710 --> 00:21:27.670
had like 500 lines of code
that just touched a Redis box,

00:21:27.670 --> 00:21:29.800
and had kind of-- whenever
you got put in a queue,

00:21:29.800 --> 00:21:31.467
depending on where
you are in the queue,

00:21:31.467 --> 00:21:33.820
it would basically say
to the client, hey, cool.

00:21:33.820 --> 00:21:36.350
Come check back in in
X number of seconds.

00:21:36.350 --> 00:21:37.740
Here's where you are in line.

00:21:37.740 --> 00:21:40.620
Because at one point, I think
we had 300,000 players in queue,

00:21:40.620 --> 00:21:42.943
and we were accepting
like 50 players

00:21:42.943 --> 00:21:44.860
a second or something
like that into the game.

00:21:44.860 --> 00:21:46.900
And so that queue
was quite long.

00:21:46.900 --> 00:21:48.910
And just the act of
checking the queue

00:21:48.910 --> 00:21:51.350
would have taken down the
service all by itself.

00:21:51.350 --> 00:21:53.892
And so we actually had to kind
of write in this graceful fall

00:21:53.892 --> 00:21:56.142
off or whatever so it'll
only check back in once every

00:21:56.142 --> 00:21:58.420
three or four minutes, when
you were more than an hour

00:21:58.420 --> 00:21:59.795
in queue, and that
kind of thing.

00:21:59.795 --> 00:22:01.212
The nice thing
about that, too, is

00:22:01.212 --> 00:22:03.820
we can also overload the
messages that the queue would

00:22:03.820 --> 00:22:04.690
tell players.

00:22:04.690 --> 00:22:06.550
Like if something was
really going wrong,

00:22:06.550 --> 00:22:08.170
we could change the
text in that box

00:22:08.170 --> 00:22:10.210
and say, hey, we're
having this other problem,

00:22:10.210 --> 00:22:12.242
so queue times are
going to be even longer,

00:22:12.242 --> 00:22:13.200
and that kind of thing.

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:14.540
And that's been
really, really helpful.

00:22:14.540 --> 00:22:15.040
MARK MANDEL: Oh, nice,

00:22:15.040 --> 00:22:16.300
JESSE HOUSTON: And then
anybody that actually made it

00:22:16.300 --> 00:22:18.543
through the queue got a
reward, these little titles

00:22:18.543 --> 00:22:19.460
that are in your game.

00:22:19.460 --> 00:22:21.668
So we also recorded all the
players who sat the queue

00:22:21.668 --> 00:22:24.370
and made it through, and so
a message that you would get,

00:22:24.370 --> 00:22:28.150
the error code, was called
QueenBee, queue blocked.

00:22:28.150 --> 00:22:30.280
And so the title that
you would get in the game

00:22:30.280 --> 00:22:31.717
is Slayer of the Queen.

00:22:31.717 --> 00:22:32.800
JON FOUST: That's awesome.

00:22:32.800 --> 00:22:35.860
So I can imagine that,
given all of the things

00:22:35.860 --> 00:22:37.870
you had to go through
at launch, that there

00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:40.420
had to be some people that
you had to reach on our end

00:22:40.420 --> 00:22:43.120
to actually help you with
the launch and any type

00:22:43.120 --> 00:22:45.110
of other integration
that you had to do.

00:22:45.110 --> 00:22:47.900
So I'm curious about how our
professional teams helped you

00:22:47.900 --> 00:22:48.400
out.

00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:49.810
And I'm not sure
exactly which teams

00:22:49.810 --> 00:22:50.977
you actually met with, but--

00:22:50.977 --> 00:22:51.850
JESSE HOUSTON: Oh.

00:22:51.850 --> 00:22:53.110
JON FOUST: --hopefully you
could speak to that just a bit.

00:22:53.110 --> 00:22:54.360
JESSE HOUSTON: Yeah, for sure.

00:22:54.360 --> 00:22:57.700
So we've had a great
relationship with the GCP folks

00:22:57.700 --> 00:22:59.260
since open beta launch.

00:22:59.260 --> 00:23:01.780
Actually, our account
manager was there

00:23:01.780 --> 00:23:04.540
when I pushed the big button
on open beta 18 months ago

00:23:04.540 --> 00:23:07.410
or whatever, and he actually
brought the champagne.

00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:10.510
And so we basically
got the opportunity

00:23:10.510 --> 00:23:15.430
to work with the CRE team,
and the GCP technical account

00:23:15.430 --> 00:23:17.710
managers, and the
service account managers,

00:23:17.710 --> 00:23:19.620
and stuff like that,
through the build up to.

00:23:19.620 --> 00:23:22.510
And that gave us a lot of
insight of what could fall over

00:23:22.510 --> 00:23:23.680
and what couldn't.

00:23:23.680 --> 00:23:26.208
The weekend before launch,
when we were running into the,

00:23:26.208 --> 00:23:28.000
we don't know why our
game isn't turning on

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:30.460
because we're DDOSing
ourselves, for example, we

00:23:30.460 --> 00:23:36.220
had five folks from CRE and from
the GCP technical team on site,

00:23:36.220 --> 00:23:38.310
and they stayed up all
night with us helping.

00:23:38.310 --> 00:23:40.030
We set up desks in the war room.

00:23:40.030 --> 00:23:42.190
We have basically a big
common space in the studio

00:23:42.190 --> 00:23:43.523
that we normally use for mo-cap.

00:23:43.523 --> 00:23:45.700
We basically set up 15 desks.

00:23:45.700 --> 00:23:48.550
And then, as launch happened,
everybody was on site

00:23:48.550 --> 00:23:50.000
with us helping call the shots.

00:23:50.000 --> 00:23:52.840
We had a dedicated
Google Hangouts PC

00:23:52.840 --> 00:23:55.370
with a big monitor that was
kind of pointing into the room

00:23:55.370 --> 00:23:57.760
that folks from Google
could phone in and help.

00:23:57.760 --> 00:23:59.260
Then, at one point,
we had something

00:23:59.260 --> 00:24:01.640
like 50 Googlers supporting
us during the launch.

00:24:01.640 --> 00:24:02.985
It was really awesome.

00:24:02.985 --> 00:24:04.360
I have the
opportunity of working

00:24:04.360 --> 00:24:06.280
on a relatively cool thing,
like working on games

00:24:06.280 --> 00:24:07.238
is kind of interesting.

00:24:07.238 --> 00:24:09.910
And so as folks kind
of heard that we

00:24:09.910 --> 00:24:11.340
were doing this thing, and--

00:24:11.340 --> 00:24:14.650
at one point, we had folks
from the GCP Linux kernel

00:24:14.650 --> 00:24:17.050
team heard that we were
having a kernel panic

00:24:17.050 --> 00:24:19.433
issue because of some reason.

00:24:19.433 --> 00:24:21.850
And next thing I know, I've
got some dude that understands

00:24:21.850 --> 00:24:23.380
how all that crazy
business works,

00:24:23.380 --> 00:24:25.820
and he's writing
kernel patches for us

00:24:25.820 --> 00:24:27.070
just because it's interesting.

00:24:27.070 --> 00:24:28.880
And he couldn't log in,
and he's like, well, I've

00:24:28.880 --> 00:24:29.922
got nothing better to do.

00:24:29.922 --> 00:24:32.080
Let me go and fix
your kernel panics.

00:24:32.080 --> 00:24:33.250
And it's like, great!

00:24:33.250 --> 00:24:33.838
Let's go!

00:24:33.838 --> 00:24:34.630
It was really wild.

00:24:34.630 --> 00:24:36.505
Folks were coming in
from all over the place.

00:24:36.505 --> 00:24:40.600
We got custom versions of the
Kubernetes Masters written

00:24:40.600 --> 00:24:42.220
for us, because we
were putting a load

00:24:42.220 --> 00:24:45.563
on the Masters like no one
at Google had ever seen.

00:24:45.563 --> 00:24:47.230
And we were basically
turning on and off

00:24:47.230 --> 00:24:49.270
so many pods per
second, at one point,

00:24:49.270 --> 00:24:52.700
our Master was getting something
like 2,500 requests per second,

00:24:52.700 --> 00:24:54.790
and it's like two
orders of magnitude

00:24:54.790 --> 00:24:56.770
bigger than Google had
ever seen before, even

00:24:56.770 --> 00:24:57.805
on their own services.

00:24:57.805 --> 00:24:58.930
And they're like, well, OK.

00:24:58.930 --> 00:25:01.690
I guess we have to make
a Snowflake version,

00:25:01.690 --> 00:25:03.200
as it was called, for you guys.

00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:04.990
And so it was super supportive.

00:25:04.990 --> 00:25:06.580
Everybody from
capacity planning--

00:25:06.580 --> 00:25:10.310
we were 3x off what our
craziest numbers were,

00:25:10.310 --> 00:25:12.580
and so we had the
capacity planning

00:25:12.580 --> 00:25:16.240
folks phoning in going,
just finding servers for us.

00:25:16.240 --> 00:25:18.370
And every time that
we would find servers,

00:25:18.370 --> 00:25:20.590
eight hours later,
the problems shifted

00:25:20.590 --> 00:25:22.192
to a new area of the world.

00:25:22.192 --> 00:25:23.650
And so they were
coming back online

00:25:23.650 --> 00:25:25.840
to find us more servers
for throughout the world.

00:25:25.840 --> 00:25:27.798
And it was a really
awesome experience overall.

00:25:27.798 --> 00:25:29.298
MARK MANDEL: Now,
that sounds great.

00:25:29.298 --> 00:25:32.230
I'd love to know, as well, even
more about that CRE experience.

00:25:32.230 --> 00:25:34.000
Were they highlighting
things that you're

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:35.480
like, this stuff is amazing?

00:25:35.480 --> 00:25:36.580
And then, were they
like, this part--

00:25:36.580 --> 00:25:37.872
they're like, hm, I don't know?

00:25:37.872 --> 00:25:39.820
Or what did that
whole thing look like

00:25:39.820 --> 00:25:40.960
as you engage with CRE?

00:25:40.960 --> 00:25:41.793
JESSE HOUSTON: Yeah.

00:25:41.793 --> 00:25:45.100
So over the six months towards
launch, they would come on site

00:25:45.100 --> 00:25:47.490
and take a look at our
tech stack with us,

00:25:47.490 --> 00:25:49.150
take a look at the
usage patterns,

00:25:49.150 --> 00:25:52.210
and go, hey, have you
thought about maybe

00:25:52.210 --> 00:25:58.283
not using Cloud SQL for all
of your off-end inventory?

00:25:58.283 --> 00:25:59.200
It's going to be fine.

00:25:59.200 --> 00:26:02.060
We load tested it to like
8 million, bazillion.

00:26:02.060 --> 00:26:03.392
And they're like, yeah, cool.

00:26:03.392 --> 00:26:04.600
No, that's not going to work.

00:26:04.600 --> 00:26:06.207
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:06.207 --> 00:26:07.540
Have you thought about Bigtable?

00:26:07.540 --> 00:26:08.040
Nah.

00:26:08.040 --> 00:26:09.610
We don't need Bigtable.

00:26:09.610 --> 00:26:11.220
The old ways, that's
the right stuff.

00:26:11.220 --> 00:26:11.720
No, no, no.

00:26:11.720 --> 00:26:11.920
No.

00:26:11.920 --> 00:26:12.850
It's not the right stuff.

00:26:12.850 --> 00:26:13.250
OK.

00:26:13.250 --> 00:26:13.720
Sure.

00:26:13.720 --> 00:26:14.220
Cool.

00:26:14.220 --> 00:26:17.110
Then, sure enough, yeah--
it was the wrong choice.

00:26:17.110 --> 00:26:18.610
Bigtable was the right way.

00:26:18.610 --> 00:26:22.940
But a lot of it was just
them coming on site,

00:26:22.940 --> 00:26:26.210
being able to leverage all of
this institutional knowledge

00:26:26.210 --> 00:26:28.540
that's built up at Google
and with other partners,

00:26:28.540 --> 00:26:31.970
and really kind of just poke
really gentle holes in what we

00:26:31.970 --> 00:26:33.620
were building in our
thought processes,

00:26:33.620 --> 00:26:35.533
and giving us a
lot more context.

00:26:35.533 --> 00:26:36.950
And we basically
just kind of came

00:26:36.950 --> 00:26:39.273
up with this very
prioritized list of stuff

00:26:39.273 --> 00:26:40.190
that we needed to fix.

00:26:40.190 --> 00:26:43.010
And the issues that
we had on May 21

00:26:43.010 --> 00:26:46.040
were just the next stuff that
we couldn't get to in time,

00:26:46.040 --> 00:26:47.440
which was really super awesome.

00:26:47.440 --> 00:26:49.190
Because the stuff that
was way above there

00:26:49.190 --> 00:26:51.170
was way harder to
fix, so stuff that we

00:26:51.170 --> 00:26:53.970
could turn around in under a
day ended up being the stuff.

00:26:53.970 --> 00:26:57.740
And so they basically
continued to pay attention

00:26:57.740 --> 00:27:00.870
through the launch, and started
projecting out going like,

00:27:00.870 --> 00:27:03.280
hey, as an example,
we were using--

00:27:03.280 --> 00:27:04.270
so, again, in SQL--

00:27:04.270 --> 00:27:06.440
SQL was the bane
of our existence.

00:27:06.440 --> 00:27:06.940
I think.

00:27:06.940 --> 00:27:10.370
We were doing binary logs
for all of our transactions.

00:27:10.370 --> 00:27:12.290
And at one point,
we got a call--

00:27:12.290 --> 00:27:14.660
I think it was on Friday
after launch-- going, cool.

00:27:14.660 --> 00:27:18.080
So you're going to run out of
hard disk space on the largest

00:27:18.080 --> 00:27:21.188
instance that Google can
make just due to binary logs.

00:27:21.188 --> 00:27:23.480
Your whole service is going
to fall over in three days.

00:27:23.480 --> 00:27:24.798
MARK MANDEL: Small problem.

00:27:24.798 --> 00:27:26.090
JESSE HOUSTON: It's like, cool.

00:27:26.090 --> 00:27:27.620
Thank god you called.

00:27:27.620 --> 00:27:28.910
We've got three days.

00:27:28.910 --> 00:27:29.630
All right.

00:27:29.630 --> 00:27:31.280
How do we fix this?

00:27:31.280 --> 00:27:33.140
And it's just been that
kind of thing where,

00:27:33.140 --> 00:27:37.760
while we were kind of eyes
deep in the immediate fire,

00:27:37.760 --> 00:27:40.310
the CRE team ended up
just kind of having that

00:27:40.310 --> 00:27:43.460
one-week-plus-out perspective,
and spent time coming up with

00:27:43.460 --> 00:27:44.900
solutions to like, oh, OK, cool.

00:27:44.900 --> 00:27:46.740
Instead of this, do
this other thing.

00:27:46.740 --> 00:27:49.520
And when it got really crazy
they opened up various editors

00:27:49.520 --> 00:27:51.063
and were supporting us directly.

00:27:51.063 --> 00:27:51.980
So it was pretty cool.

00:27:51.980 --> 00:27:52.700
JON FOUST: That sounds awesome.

00:27:52.700 --> 00:27:54.470
Glad you could cooperate
with a lot of people.

00:27:54.470 --> 00:27:56.762
And it's kind of funny how
games bring people together,

00:27:56.762 --> 00:27:57.840
isn't it?

00:27:57.840 --> 00:27:59.483
JESSE HOUSTON: Right.

00:27:59.483 --> 00:28:01.400
We always talk about
that in the product side,

00:28:01.400 --> 00:28:03.233
but never in the
development side.

00:28:03.233 --> 00:28:05.150
At least, none of the
companies that I've ever

00:28:05.150 --> 00:28:08.360
worked with before had
the presence of mind,

00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:11.940
or the openness to invite
other companies to support,

00:28:11.940 --> 00:28:14.783
and it was really sweet getting
to work with Google on the fact

00:28:14.783 --> 00:28:16.700
that, because they're
just like, we'll happily

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:18.950
come into your house and
help you, just open the door.

00:28:18.950 --> 00:28:20.200
And we're like, yeah, come on.

00:28:20.200 --> 00:28:21.001
Please.

00:28:21.001 --> 00:28:22.707
JON FOUST: Just wipe your feet.

00:28:22.707 --> 00:28:23.540
JESSE HOUSTON: Yeah.

00:28:23.540 --> 00:28:24.410
Right.

00:28:24.410 --> 00:28:25.250
Come on in.

00:28:25.250 --> 00:28:25.850
I beg you.

00:28:25.850 --> 00:28:27.377
So it was a good experience.

00:28:27.377 --> 00:28:28.460
JON FOUST: That's awesome.

00:28:28.460 --> 00:28:31.430
So I'm curious-- if you
could do this all again--

00:28:31.430 --> 00:28:33.447
I mean, granted you let
us into your house--

00:28:33.447 --> 00:28:34.280
JESSE HOUSTON: Yeah.

00:28:34.280 --> 00:28:35.860
JON FOUST: What is the one thing
that you would do differently?

00:28:35.860 --> 00:28:37.527
What is the one thing
you would possibly

00:28:37.527 --> 00:28:38.920
change if you have something?

00:28:38.920 --> 00:28:40.550
JESSE HOUSTON: You know, that's
such a weird loaded question,

00:28:40.550 --> 00:28:41.360
because--

00:28:41.360 --> 00:28:43.040
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:43.040 --> 00:28:46.340
--the way I look at that is
like, what didn't we see?

00:28:46.340 --> 00:28:49.580
The assumption there is
that I'm smarter today

00:28:49.580 --> 00:28:52.610
than I was yesterday, or that
I had inherently bad judgment

00:28:52.610 --> 00:28:53.110
then.

00:28:53.110 --> 00:28:54.530
But I don't think I would
have changed anything.

00:28:54.530 --> 00:28:55.947
Given the knowledge
that we had, I

00:28:55.947 --> 00:28:57.590
think we did a really good job.

00:28:57.590 --> 00:29:01.880
I think if I could
have seen or learned

00:29:01.880 --> 00:29:03.860
something ahead
of time, I think I

00:29:03.860 --> 00:29:06.500
would have liked to have done a
better job with log management.

00:29:06.500 --> 00:29:08.860
I know that's really
explicit, but there

00:29:08.860 --> 00:29:10.370
were a couple of
things that took

00:29:10.370 --> 00:29:13.400
a little longer for
us to identify because

00:29:13.400 --> 00:29:15.270
of how we did log management.

00:29:15.270 --> 00:29:16.978
As an example, it took
a long time for us

00:29:16.978 --> 00:29:19.103
to load testing back online,
because we didn't know

00:29:19.103 --> 00:29:20.420
that we were DDOSing ourselves.

00:29:20.420 --> 00:29:22.383
That was in the logs
from the first outage.

00:29:22.383 --> 00:29:23.300
We just didn't see it.

00:29:23.300 --> 00:29:24.633
We didn't know what to look for.

00:29:24.633 --> 00:29:27.170
We didn't know what to
read, these 415 errors

00:29:27.170 --> 00:29:28.550
that were getting spat out.

00:29:28.550 --> 00:29:29.570
It wasn't until--

00:29:29.570 --> 00:29:31.190
I think it was Sunday
night or Monday,

00:29:31.190 --> 00:29:32.720
really, really early
in the morning,

00:29:32.720 --> 00:29:35.128
that one of our engineers
was like, what's error 415?

00:29:35.128 --> 00:29:36.170
It's like, I don't know--

00:29:36.170 --> 00:29:38.290
Google search away--
and, oh, [BLEEP]..

00:29:38.290 --> 00:29:40.703
[LAUGHTER]

00:29:40.703 --> 00:29:42.620
But it took multiple
days before we got to it,

00:29:42.620 --> 00:29:43.745
because we were just like--

00:29:43.745 --> 00:29:45.360
we just didn't do
a good job there.

00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:48.470
The other thing that I think I
would do personally differently

00:29:48.470 --> 00:29:49.560
is--

00:29:49.560 --> 00:29:52.940
I don't think my
career really taught me

00:29:52.940 --> 00:29:54.462
a lot about crisis management.

00:29:54.462 --> 00:29:55.670
MARK MANDEL: Oh, interesting.

00:29:55.670 --> 00:29:58.730
JESSE HOUSTON: And so
in the minutes of sleep

00:29:58.730 --> 00:30:00.800
that I was getting, or
the minutes of downtime

00:30:00.800 --> 00:30:02.390
that I was getting
post launch, I

00:30:02.390 --> 00:30:06.230
was doing a lot of reading
about how firefighters actually

00:30:06.230 --> 00:30:07.340
manage crisis.

00:30:07.340 --> 00:30:11.630
And even just that small
amount of learning that I

00:30:11.630 --> 00:30:14.000
got in that week in terms of--

00:30:14.000 --> 00:30:14.850
I noticed a thing.

00:30:14.850 --> 00:30:15.710
OK, cool.

00:30:15.710 --> 00:30:16.810
Who does this really well?

00:30:16.810 --> 00:30:17.810
OK, firefighters do.

00:30:17.810 --> 00:30:19.700
OK, quick-- go find
literally a textbook

00:30:19.700 --> 00:30:21.800
on how to be a fire
captain, and how

00:30:21.800 --> 00:30:24.440
to think about how to
put a burning house out,

00:30:24.440 --> 00:30:26.710
and how to tell folks to
go and do the right work.

00:30:26.710 --> 00:30:30.180
Just that a little bit of extra
knowledge was really critical.

00:30:30.180 --> 00:30:33.650
And if I could have learned
something ahead of time

00:30:33.650 --> 00:30:35.637
that I'm more
intelligent about now,

00:30:35.637 --> 00:30:37.220
I would have liked
to have been better

00:30:37.220 --> 00:30:41.540
at crisis management, just as
a general leadership trait.

00:30:41.540 --> 00:30:46.430
A lot of leadership folks and
technical leadership manuals

00:30:46.430 --> 00:30:48.200
and blog posts, and
all this other stuff,

00:30:48.200 --> 00:30:52.790
talk a lot about
vision and empowerment,

00:30:52.790 --> 00:30:58.130
and all this other stuff, that I
ascribe to really aggressively.

00:30:58.130 --> 00:31:00.000
But on the other hand, when--

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:02.000
for lack of a better
term-- when [BLEEP] hitting

00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:05.210
the fan and the building's
literally on fire,

00:31:05.210 --> 00:31:07.310
that's a very
different set of skills

00:31:07.310 --> 00:31:10.610
than trying to expose a
vision, and getting up

00:31:10.610 --> 00:31:12.420
in front of teams, and
this kind of stuff.

00:31:12.420 --> 00:31:14.795
And it definitely highlighted
a personal weakness for me,

00:31:14.795 --> 00:31:17.570
where just literally
keeping folks

00:31:17.570 --> 00:31:20.540
on task, on the right
stuff, and not accidentally

00:31:20.540 --> 00:31:23.090
get people into a flail-y
place, and that kind of stuff.

00:31:23.090 --> 00:31:26.420
I'm continuing to invest more
of my personal growth time

00:31:26.420 --> 00:31:27.560
on that kind of stuff.

00:31:27.560 --> 00:31:28.460
JON FOUST: Awesome.

