00:34:16.719 --> 00:34:18.300
So our question of
the week is, what

00:34:18.300 --> 00:34:20.741
is the difference between
Premium and Standard network?

00:34:20.741 --> 00:34:22.449
MARK MANDEL: Oh, this
is a good question.

00:34:22.449 --> 00:34:23.074
So this is fun.

00:34:23.074 --> 00:34:27.219
So way back in the day,
there was but one network.

00:34:27.219 --> 00:34:29.372
And there was only
one network to choose.

00:34:29.372 --> 00:34:31.830
And that was what we currently
refer to now as Premium Tier

00:34:31.830 --> 00:34:32.460
networking.

00:34:32.460 --> 00:34:34.530
So the short answer
here is the way

00:34:34.530 --> 00:34:38.850
Google's network works is we try
and get you on to our network

00:34:38.850 --> 00:34:39.907
as fast as possible.

00:34:39.907 --> 00:34:41.449
So we have how much
of our own fiber,

00:34:41.449 --> 00:34:43.020
and that runs under the
water, and all that kind

00:34:43.020 --> 00:34:43.663
of cool stuff.

00:34:43.663 --> 00:34:46.080
So we basically try and get
you from your internet service

00:34:46.080 --> 00:34:47.955
provider directly to
our points of presence--

00:34:47.955 --> 00:34:49.500
we have over 100
around the world--

00:34:49.500 --> 00:34:51.330
and then get you inside
the Google network,

00:34:51.330 --> 00:34:52.350
where we can control everything.

00:34:52.350 --> 00:34:54.840
It runs over our own network,
and it works really nicely.

00:34:54.840 --> 00:34:55.960
This is the Premium Tier.

00:34:55.960 --> 00:34:57.960
This is basically high
performance routing.

00:34:57.960 --> 00:35:00.252
We're able to do some other
things, like have our Cloud

00:35:00.252 --> 00:35:01.450
load balancing to be global.

00:35:01.450 --> 00:35:03.233
I have global SLAs,
things like that.

00:35:03.233 --> 00:35:05.650
This is basically what you
want when you want performance,

00:35:05.650 --> 00:35:06.583
super fast.

00:35:06.583 --> 00:35:09.000
But what we also have is what
we call Standard Tier, which

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:11.250
is very similar to basically
what a lot of other cloud

00:35:11.250 --> 00:35:13.320
providers provide,
wherein you spend

00:35:13.320 --> 00:35:17.395
the vast majority of your
time over the public internet.

00:35:17.395 --> 00:35:19.020
So you're not running
over our network.

00:35:19.020 --> 00:35:19.800
It's just going to
be a little slower.

00:35:19.800 --> 00:35:20.760
You're going to have
a little bit less

00:35:20.760 --> 00:35:22.320
of a consistent experience.

00:35:22.320 --> 00:35:23.650
But it's cheaper.

00:35:23.650 --> 00:35:25.380
This is where we're not going to
try and get you on our network

00:35:25.380 --> 00:35:26.140
as fast as possible.

00:35:26.140 --> 00:35:27.750
We're going to leave you
on the public network.

00:35:27.750 --> 00:35:29.700
And so you're going to be
on a low performance tier,

00:35:29.700 --> 00:35:30.450
but it is cheaper.

00:35:30.450 --> 00:35:33.720
So maybe if cost is what
you're trying to optimize for,

00:35:33.720 --> 00:35:35.650
the Standard Tier
could be really good.

00:35:35.650 --> 00:35:36.940
But if speed is what
you're looking for,

00:35:36.940 --> 00:35:38.732
then the Premium Tier
could be really good.

00:35:38.732 --> 00:35:41.970
And you can choose either one
as you see fit for each workload

00:35:41.970 --> 00:35:42.870
as you require it.

00:35:42.870 --> 00:35:45.228
So it really depends
on what your needs are.

00:35:45.228 --> 00:35:46.020
JON FOUST: Awesome.

